WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.720
When Tim Berners-Lee pr…

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:06.920
When Tim Berners-Lee prop…

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:10.800
When T…

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:16.320
When Tim Bern…

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:17.280
oh come on!

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:24.800
When Tim Berners-Lee proposed the idea&nbsp;
for the World Wide Web back in 1989,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:29.600
he wrote about a “universal linked&nbsp;
information system”. His exact words:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:32.640
“a place to be found for any&nbsp;
information or reference which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:36.000
one felt was important, and a&nbsp;
way of finding it afterwards”.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:43.120
Three decades later, and you’ve possibly&nbsp;
noticed that the web is a bit of a mess.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:46.880
What happened to that&nbsp;
universal information system?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:49.760
What even is a cookie, and&nbsp;
why do we need to accept them?

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:53.760
There are two reasons that&nbsp;
the web is terrible right now:

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:57.840
first, advertisers. And second, people.

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:01.200
...I actually just realised that I just
implied that

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.040
advertisers are not people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:05.360
and there’s some really interesting stuff
about corporate&nbsp;personhood

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:07.840
and, employees acting as a
hive-mind there, and--

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:10.800
Anyway! Let’s talk&nbsp;
about the advertisers.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:14.880
The first versions of the web&nbsp;
were simple. Text and images&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:19.440
and links to other text and images.&nbsp;
You could use forms to send information,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:23.464
but the whole thing was ‘stateless’.
This means neither the web browser

00:01:23.464 --> 00:01:26.324
nor the web server kept track of you
from one page to the next:

00:01:26.324 --> 00:01:29.781
you just sent a request for a document,
you got the document back,

00:01:29.781 --> 00:01:32.567
and&nbsp;that was it. If you submitted a form,

00:01:32.567 --> 00:01:35.653
that one response could be&nbsp;
tailored, but after that, nope,

00:01:35.653 --> 00:01:38.636
the server’s forgotten&nbsp;about you
because it can’t tell you apart

00:01:38.636 --> 00:01:42.081
from anyone&nbsp;else who’s using the same network.

00:01:42.081 --> 00:01:45.120
Now this wasn't, long-term, a great plan.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.760
In theory you could hack together&nbsp;
something more complicated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:52.204
send forms back and forth, and people&nbsp;
did build things like that,

00:01:52.204 --> 00:01:54.451
but they’d break very, very easily.

00:01:54.451 --> 00:01:58.691
It was impossible to have something&nbsp;
both reliable and personalised.

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:02.240
Then in 1994, a programmer called&nbsp;
Lou Montulli, who was building a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:06.480
web browser at a company called&nbsp;
Netscape, came up with “cookies”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:10.400
adapting an old computer science&nbsp;
technique called “magic cookies”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:14.640
They were so useful that every other&nbsp;
browser started to use them as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:17.120
and they got built&nbsp;
into the web standards.

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:22.800
A cookie is “client-side storage”:&nbsp;
your web browser sends the request to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.830
the server, as usual, but the server sends back&nbsp;
two things:

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:27.555
the page you asked for,

00:02:27.555 --> 00:02:32.158
and a little text file called a cookie&nbsp;
that your browser then saves.

00:02:32.158 --> 00:02:36.640
After it’s saved, every time that browser&nbsp;
makes a request to that same server,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:41.120
it sends the cookie along with it.&nbsp;
So that cookie could contain your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:45.301
preferences: if you choose “dark mode”,&nbsp;
then the browser would now send a

00:02:45.301 --> 00:02:49.197
“dark mode” preference, in that cookie,
every single request.

00:02:49.197 --> 00:02:51.767
If the server asked for it,
then that cookie would last

00:02:51.767 --> 00:02:54.800
even if you closed the&nbsp;browser,
and restarted your computer.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:57.756
For years and years and years.

00:02:57.756 --> 00:03:00.720
Or a cookie could be used for&nbsp;
authentication: so after you’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:03.670
logged into a site, that site gives your&nbsp;
browser a cookie

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:06.405
that’s just a long, random string of characters.

00:03:06.405 --> 00:03:11.714
But because no-one except the server and that cookie
have that long string of characters,

00:03:11.714 --> 00:03:16.640
it can act like a password: the browser sends it
back along&nbsp;with every page request,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:20.320
and the server looks at it and&nbsp;
goes “oh, yeah, I remember you”.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:26.480
The designers of cookies&nbsp;looked at that,
and they knew that they could be used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:30.160
to track people, so there was a&nbsp;
very, very important rule for privacy:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:36.560
from the very start, only the site that set the&nbsp;
cookie can read the cookie back.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:40.160
Except. Because of the way the web&nbsp;
is built, sites can include material&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:43.760
from other servers. A web page can&nbsp;
pull in an image from somewhere else,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:47.440
or some scripting code, or even an&nbsp;
entire other web site, included in a&nbsp;frame.

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:52.720
And those other servers could&nbsp;
all set and read their own cookies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:55.200
“Third-party cookies”, they were called.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:01.360
So when web pages included advertising,&nbsp;
well, those adverts came from the advertising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:05.680
company’s servers, so those advertisers&nbsp;
could set a cookie that tracked people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:10.080
across the entire web. If the same&nbsp;
ad company partnered with loads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:15.440
of different web sites, they could start&nbsp;
building up some very detailed profiles.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:20.160
No, they might not know who you&nbsp;
are at first. But they knew you visited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:23.840
that web site about your hometown,&nbsp;then
you visited that web site about your college,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:28.494
and then you visited that web site about
a very specific&nbsp; medical condition.

00:04:28.494 --> 00:04:30.915
Oh, and then you bought something
from an online shop,

00:04:30.915 --> 00:04:32.960
so they can work behind-the-scenes

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:35.381
combine the details from that shop
and their cookies,

00:04:35.381 --> 00:04:38.800
and add your name and address
to the profile.

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:42.000
All those fancy “share with&nbsp;Facebook”
buttons? Well, yeah, loads of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.495
could report back to Facebook where
you were going.

00:04:44.495 --> 00:04:49.088
"Hey, this person's looking at all these sites!
So now you can tailor adverts for them!"

00:04:49.088 --> 00:04:53.353
Which&nbsp;is why you could be on a site that seems
to be completely disconnected from Facebook,

00:04:53.353 --> 00:04:58.880
and still see adverts that were uncannily linked to&nbsp;
things you’ve been thinking about.

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:03.760
It took a long while before&nbsp;
regulators decided that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:07.920
maybe, advertising companies having&nbsp;
enormous databases on basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:12.400
everything that people were thinking&nbsp;
might be a bit of a privacy problem.

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:15.440
The European Union acted&nbsp;
first. The “Cookie Law”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:20.103
which was the euphemistic name for, um,
this mess...

00:05:20.103 --> 00:05:23.360
meant that all the EU member&nbsp;
countries had to put a law in place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:26.735
requiring consent to place cookies.

00:05:26.735 --> 00:05:28.808
That wasn’t particularly well-thought-out,

00:05:28.808 --> 00:05:31.689
because “consent” requires clicking&nbsp;OK,

00:05:31.689 --> 00:05:34.770
and if there’s a confusing box
in between the average person

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:37.151
and the article they want to read,

00:05:37.151 --> 00:05:40.000
look, nearly everyone is
just going to just click OK.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:43.120
And by this point, browser makers&nbsp;
were starting to give users options&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:47.120
to block third-party cookies anyway,
so advertisers were adjusting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:50.450
they were tracking people not through cookies,
but&nbsp; through “fingerprinting”:

00:05:50.450 --> 00:05:54.784
looking at all the apparently-innocent
individual quirks

00:05:54.784 --> 00:05:58.815
of how that exact&nbsp;computer was set up
and how it rendered a web page.

00:05:58.815 --> 00:06:03.005
The fonts you had installed, the size of your monitor,&nbsp;
the web sites you've visited in the past,

00:06:03.005 --> 00:06:07.249
all things that individually appear&nbsp;
innocent and safe but which together

00:06:07.249 --> 00:06:10.111
uniquely identify each person.

00:06:10.111 --> 00:06:14.670
Browser&nbsp;makers and advertisers ended up in
an odd war with each other about&nbsp;privacy:

00:06:14.670 --> 00:06:18.675
occasionally a civil war, given that one of the&nbsp;
most popular browsers

00:06:18.675 --> 00:06:23.040
and also one of the&nbsp;major advertising companies

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:26.560
were under the same corporate umbrella.

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:30.080
So later, the General Data&nbsp;
Protection Regulation, GDPR,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:33.067
put even stronger requirements in&nbsp;
place, at least for EU citizens.

00:06:33.067 --> 00:06:37.840
Not just “no cookies without consent”:
no&nbsp; tracking without consent. At all times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:40.749
the user must have full knowledge&nbsp;
what they are signing up for,

00:06:40.749 --> 00:06:44.151
and must consent to it.
Even if the company’s not&nbsp;in the EU.

00:06:44.151 --> 00:06:47.770
Even if they’re storing data
on EU citizens "outside",

00:06:47.770 --> 00:06:48.891
it still applies to them.

00:06:48.891 --> 00:06:53.049
The potential maximum fine is 4% of the
company’s global annual revenue.

00:06:53.049 --> 00:06:54.565
Not profit, revenue.

00:06:54.565 --> 00:06:58.016
Or €20 million, whichever is higher.

00:06:58.016 --> 00:07:02.023
And yeah, sure, maybe the EU couldn’t
easily fine an all-American&nbsp;company,

00:07:02.023 --> 00:07:06.240
but they can definitely&nbsp;stop them
doing business in Europe if they don't pay.

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:08.753
So all the advertisers had two options.

00:07:08.753 --> 00:07:11.477
Number one: stop tracking people!

00:07:11.477 --> 00:07:15.738
Or number two, add a big box on each&nbsp;page
saying “hey, is it OK if we track you?”

00:07:15.738 --> 00:07:18.365
And legally, you should&nbsp;
be able to easily say no

00:07:18.365 --> 00:07:22.329
and still access the site. Opting out should&nbsp;
be a simple, one-click process.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.329 --> 00:07:26.601
And anyone who’s been on the web&nbsp;
lately knows that’s often not true.

00:07:26.601 --> 00:07:31.320
So, yes, at least some of those&nbsp;popups
and frustrations are because of&nbsp;advertisers.

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:34.800
Certainly, they normalised&nbsp;
the idea of intrusive popup boxes.

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:38.720
But why can they put those&nbsp;
boxes there in the first place?

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:43.200
The web is built around freedom&nbsp;
of design. It’s one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:48.000
fundamental principles: within the&nbsp;
box that the web browser gives you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:51.120
a designer can put almost anything.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:53.335
For decade after decade after decade,

00:07:53.335 --> 00:07:56.326
programmers&nbsp;have pushed the limits
of what’s possible:

00:07:56.326 --> 00:08:01.203
someone has even managed to&nbsp;emulate an
entire Windows 95 computer in the web browser.

00:08:01.203 --> 00:08:04.570
I don’t mean just&nbsp;making a web page
look like Windows 95,

00:08:04.570 --> 00:08:09.701
I mean an entire virtual machine&nbsp;that thinks
it’s running on regular computer hardware

00:08:09.701 --> 00:08:13.920
but is actually just&nbsp;simulated
in code, in a web browser.

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:15.360
And that's incredible!

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:18.785
Give people power to create and&nbsp;
some will make amazing things.

00:08:18.785 --> 00:08:22.573
But some will look at the ability&nbsp;
to run almost any code

00:08:22.573 --> 00:08:26.256
and put&nbsp;almost anything on screen,
at least within that&nbsp;box, and go

00:08:26.256 --> 00:08:29.823
“maybe I can get some more newsletter signups
if I interrupt&nbsp;the reader”.

00:08:29.823 --> 00:08:33.943
Or "maybe I can make people’s browsers
mine cryptocurrency in the&nbsp;background

00:08:33.943 --> 00:08:35.360
"while they’re on my site!"

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:39.219
So the war starts again.

00:08:39.219 --> 00:08:42.924
Most web&nbsp;browsers now have a separate view
that you can switch into,

00:08:42.924 --> 00:08:44.760
that shows just&nbsp;
the text you want.

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:47.350
It might be called “article view” or “reader mode”.

00:08:47.350 --> 00:08:51.828
The browser tries to figure out what the main content is,
what the user actually wants to see,

00:08:51.828 --> 00:08:53.871
and&nbsp;puts just that text on screen.

00:08:53.871 --> 00:08:57.200
Stripping out the adverts,&nbsp;
removing all the popup boxes, and,

00:08:57.200 --> 00:09:00.560
yes, losing all the creativity&nbsp;
and design that the web allows.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.560 --> 00:09:05.520
But that ‘reader view’ is a lot closer&nbsp;
to Tim Berners-Lee’s original design:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:08.560
a place for information, and&nbsp;
a way to find it afterwards.

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:13.120
Of course, there are already techniques&nbsp;
to detect and defeat reader view,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:17.680
and make sure that the user has to see the&nbsp;
adverts, or the newsletter popups, or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:18.560
whatever else.

00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:23.760
Every bit of freedom that a&nbsp;
designer has for creativity and good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:27.360
is also freedom for abuse.&nbsp;
That’s true not just on the web,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:30.240
but in every single aspect&nbsp;
of the online world.

